@meta (version: "1.0", title: "The interactive document field guide", locale: "en-US", currency: "USD")

# Seven ways to explore data

![Open notebook and loose pages in blue tones](assets/3c680559948c017563e8324107dd8d63dac5fb2b49ae347afffe8510fd39e0e0.webp)

This is a complete tour of the document language in one place. All data is
fictional and local. Change a control, select a chart value, open the data
alternatives, and then read the source to see how ordinary Markdown and tinta
work together.

## Controls and live values

@let baseline = 15
@slider factor = 1 (min: 1, max: 3, label: "Value multiplier")
@slider selected_period = 1 (min: 1, max: 4, label: "Period")
@choice selected_format = "Text" (options: ["Text", "Audio", "Video"], label: "Format")
@toggle show_notes = false (label: "Show the worked notes")

@data week
period:number | actual:number | target:number
1 | 12 | 15
2 | 18 | 15
3 | 26 | 15
4 | 22 | 15
@end

@data formats
format:text | readers:number
Text | 48
Audio | 32
Video | 20
@end

@data sessions
minutes:number | pages:number
12 | 4
18 | 8
18 | 7
25 | 11
40 | 18
52 | 24
@end

@view above_target = week (
  where: row.actual * factor >= baseline,
  sort: "period",
  descending: false,
)
@calc total_actual = sum(week, "actual")
@calc selected_status = if(factor > 1, "scaled", "original")

The original weekly total is **{{ total_actual }} readings**. After multiplying
each period by **{{ factor }}**, **{{ count(above_target) }} periods** meet the
threshold. A view filters rows without changing the embedded source table.

@details "See the filtered table"
@table above_target (title: "Periods meeting the threshold")
@end

@when show_notes
### A worked note

A named value sets the threshold at {{ baseline }}. A calculation derives the
original total; the view changes when the multiplier changes. The table and charts use the same data.
@end

## Lines
@chart line week (x: "period", y: ["actual", "target"], select: selected_period, title: "Actual and target by period", xLabel: "Period", yLabel: "Readings")

## Areas
@chart area week (x: "period", y: row.actual * factor, select: selected_period, title: "Scaled actual readings", xLabel: "Period", yLabel: "Readings")

## Grouped bars
@chart bar week (x: "period", y: ["actual", "target"], select: selected_period, title: "Actual beside target", xLabel: "Period", yLabel: "Readings")

The selected period is **{{ selected_period }}**. Selecting a value in any of
these three figures changes the same slider and highlight.

## Scatter
@chart scatter sessions (x: "minutes", y: "pages", title: "Session length and pages", xLabel: "Minutes", yLabel: "Pages")

## Pie chart
@chart pie formats (x: "format", y: "readers", select: selected_format, title: "Formats as sectors")

## Donut chart
@chart donut formats (x: "format", y: "readers", select: selected_format, title: "Formats as a ring")

The selected format is **{{ selected_format }}**. Both figures share the choice
above. Their legends can hide categories for inspection without changing the
source totals.

## Histogram
@chart histogram sessions (x: "minutes", bins: 4, title: "Distribution of session lengths", xLabel: "Minutes", yLabel: "Sessions")

The histogram counts observations in intervals; it does not select individual
rows. Every figure offers a keyboard explorer and a **Show data** table.

## Expressions and formatting

The built-in functions can appear in prose as well as calculations:

| Question | Result |
| --- | --- |
| Choose by condition | {{ if(factor > 1, "Scaled", "Original") }} |
| Smaller and larger | {{ min(factor, 2) }} and {{ max(factor, 2) }} |
| Rounded division | {{ round(10 / 3, 2) }} |
| Exponent | {{ pow(factor, 2) }} |
| Row count | {{ count(week) }} |
| Column sum and mean | {{ sum(week, "actual") }} and {{ mean(week, "actual") }} |
| Fixed decimals | {{ fixed(mean(week, "actual"), 1) }} |
| Percentage | {{ percent(factor / 3) }} |
| Currency | {{ money(total_actual) }} |

## Markdown remains the foundation

- [x] **Strong text**, *emphasis*, and a [link back to the controls](#tinta-controls-and-live-values) are ordinary Markdown.
- [ ] Try ~~crossed-out text~~, tables, and a nested list:
  - A nested item stays part of the document flow.

```text
This fenced code is literal, not a live control:
@slider imaginary = 9
{{ imaginary }}
```

@details "What this tour leaves outside the document"
tinta studio can edit and save the source, import local CSV/JSON and raster images,
keep local recovery copies, and export an interactive or static HTML file.
Those are tool actions, not commands inside a document. An interactive export
contains hidden branches and all embedded data; check it before sharing a copy.
@end
